      "decoded_text": "Okay, let's see. The problem is about a warehouse starting with 120 crates on Monday morning. Then, from Monday to Thursday, they receive 35 new crates each morning and ship out 20 each evening. On Friday, they don't get any new crates and ship out one-third of all crates. We need to find out how many crates are left at the end of Friday.\n\nFirst, let's break it down day by day. Starting with Monday. The warehouse has 120 crates in the morning. Then, each morning from Monday to Thursday, they add 35 crates. Each evening, they ship out 20 crates. So, each day, the net change is +35 -20 = +15 crates. But wait, does the shipping happen after the new crates are added? The problem says \"receives 35 new crates each morning and ships out 20 crates each evening.\" So, the order is: morning receive, then evening ship. So each day, the crates increase by 35 in the morning, then decrease by 20 in the evening. So net +15 per day for four days (Monday to Thursday).\n\nBut wait, let's check the timing. Let's take Monday morning: start with 120. Then, in the morning, receive 35, so total becomes 120 +35 = 155. Then, in the evening, ship out 20, so 155 -20 = 135. So end of Monday: 135.\n\nThen Tuesday morning: start with 135. Add 35, so 170. Then ship out 20, so 150. End of Tuesday: 150.\n\nWednesday morning: 150 +35 = 185. Ship out 20: 165. End of Wednesday: 165.\n\nThursday morning: 165 +35 = 190. Ship out 20: 170. End of Thursday: 170.\n\nSo after Thursday evening, there are 170 crates.\n\nNow, Friday comes. On Friday, they receive no new crates. So the morning starts with 170. Then, in the evening, they ship out one-third of all crates. So one-third of 170 is 170 /3 ≈ 56.666... But since you can't ship a fraction of a crate, do we round down or up? The problem says \"ships out one-third of all crates currently in the warehouse.\" So probably, it's exact division. But 170 divided by 3 is 56.666..., which isn't a whole number. Hmm. Wait, maybe the problem